A||||6||BBBAD GT||Brake Line Loc & Prop Valve Questions||||5||
Z||000000||BBBAD GT||03-17-2000||09:12 AM||||Got most of my GA brake stuff, and will start it this weekend.  My question is to the guys who have put a line loc and prop valve on.  It looks like the best place to put both is inbetween the MC and the distribution block (front lines).  In the Mustang, I have a line loc inbetween the two with no problems, but all cars are different (the Mustang has a goofy brake line routing).  Is this the right place to put them?  Also, I'm assuming since the Fiero has most of its weight in the back, you would want more braking back there, so I'm assuming the prop valve should be tied to the front lines, to decrease the pressure.  <P>Also, I've heard people putting 94 full size Blazer MC on, and just recently S-10 MC with the swaps.  What yr S-10 MC are you guys going with?  I'm thinking I can do the MC swap some other time, but would like more info on it.  Thanks for the help.<P> <A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||03-17-2000||06:42 PM||||I strongly sugjest you learn more about how brakes work and what happens when you stop a car before making brake changes you read on the internet.<P>resting weight distribution is far diferant from both braking and accelerating weight distribution.<P>You Don't put the prop valve into the front brakes. Weight transfers to the front durring braking. the front wheels do far more braking than the rear.<P>It's also not a distribution block in the lines, at least in most cars. It's a ballance detect and proportioning valve. ((In some GM cars that have cross fed brakes there are 2 prop valves.)) The ballance is what trips the brake light on the dash when one circuit is low pressure. The prop valve(s) which is in the line(s) down stream of the ballance limmits pressure to the rear slaves/callipers. DO NOT install a second prop valve in the lines. You need to replace the whole balance/valve block with an adjustable unit.<P>As for line locks, These are not an acceptable alternative for a parking brake. Leaving the brakes locked at pressure for extended periods of time is not at all good for the system.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||BBBAD GT||03-17-2000||07:18 PM||||I've been learning about the function of the prop valve all day, and had the theory of them backwards/wrong.  I'm going to leave it off for now, and see how the stock one works.  One option I could have is to bypass the rear stock prop valve, and run the adj one.  Not sure how it would effect the sensor tho. <P>I don't expect to run the line lock in replace of the parking brake when the car is off, only when I could be stopped up a hill and need a secondary method of holding the car when starting.  The solenoid wouldn't  last too long if you engage it for extened periods of time.  <P>Thanks for the brake lesson.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>I'll tell ya how it turns out.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||03-17-2000||07:37 PM||||You can't bypass the ballance. Well you can if you don't want the dash light to ever work right. Something that's not a real good idea.<P>leave the stock lines allone for now. Most people tho seem to want to make a change one way or another after the upgrade.<P>In reality you got the wrong adjustable vavle it sounds like. The correct one for this application would replace the whole block.<P>If you do have to install the one you have you would be best off taking the prop valve out of the balance block and installing your valve down stream from the block.<P>I usually have installed line locks after the ballance. if you put the upstream then the line pressure trips the brake light on and you sometimes have trouble getting the thing to go off. You can also blow the internal seals in the balance.<P>The reason people change the MC is to increase the volume of the MC proportional to the increase in volume of the new slaves. The original one works, but you get more pedal travel which most people don't like.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 03-17-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||lawrence||03-17-2000||08:11 PM||||I've done quite a bit of brake work on my car.  Although it's an 88, many things are the same (car weights the same, same distribution, etc.)  I wrote an article on the brake upgrade I did, and there's a section on the combo valve.  Click the link and go to the Archives to find that article.<P>Also, on the Links page, go to the DanCar site.  David has done a lot of brake work that might give you some more insight on the braking system.<P>Good luck,<P>-L<BR> <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000005||lowCG||03-17-2000||08:12 PM||||Hey Ogre,thanks for the info on the GM proportioning situation,was wondering just how to go about that on my car,before I tear things all apart I like to have a workable plan.<BR>I figured the piston area of the calipers I have(4X40mm per wheel),and the '94 Blazer diesel gets me almost perfectly the same ratio as the stock arrangement.||||reg||1
Z||000006||BBBAD GT||03-17-2000||11:56 PM||||Ok, now I understand what you were saying about more pressure on one side if the balance block only sees one side of pressure.  I'm still getting use to this GM stuff.<P>Also, if you do install the line locks after the balancer block, I suppose you Y the two front lines coming out of the balancer into the line lock, then Y it back out to both ends of the brakes. Correct?<BR> <p>[This message has been edited by BBBAD GT (edited 03-18-2000).]||||reg||1
